Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

/*****************************************************************************
    jitSlotT4 -- BATCH TWO OF THE SWEEP, AND THE MILESTONE. 2026-08-17.

    The last four of the strict binary/comparison population migrate: !=, +, -
    and /. With the six before them THE STRICT SWEEP CLOSES AT 10 OF 10.

    ⚠ THAT IS NOT THE SWEEP CLOSING. Ops remain without slots -- they are
    out-by-SHAPE, not unswept: jitEmitDot and jitEmitRem take a third argument,
    three are jitEmitUnary, and jitEmitAssign is a shape fit parked for other
    reasons. So the null slot still means not-yet-migrated and NEVER-NULL STAYS
    OPEN. The sweep-close obligations in docs/jitSlotMigration.md are unclaimed.

    ⚠⚠ EACH CERTIFIED OP APPEARS EXACTLY ONCE IN THE ACTION, AND THAT IS A
    DESIGN CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A COINCIDENCE. Plus and != are ordinary
    fixture idiom -- they turn up in setup lines, guards and counters without
    anyone thinking about it -- so a rung certifying them can very easily use
    one incidentally and assert a count it cannot account for. The count here
    is FOUR and every one of the four is named:

        jsdOut = jsdA + jsdB        the +
        jsdOut = jsdOut - jsdC      the -
        jsdOut = jsdOut / jsdD      the /
        if jsdOut != jsdZ           the !=

    The assignments are opAssign, which is NOT migrated (it is the parked
    jitEmitAssign wrinkle), so they contribute nothing to the count. The same
    rule that kept jitSlotT3 free of arithmetic keeps this one free of a second
    occurrence of anything.

    ⚠ BOTH HALVES OF THE ACTION ARE LOAD-BEARING, which is what stops the count
    from being the only live row:

        fire 1  20 10 6 2, Z 12   ->  30, 24, 12   12 != 12 is FALSE  =>  12
        fire 2  40 10 5 5, Z 12   ->  50, 45,  9    9 != 12 is TRUE   =>  99

    If the three arithmetic ops were wrong, fire 1 would not be 12. If != were
    wrong, fire 2 would not reach 99. 12 and 99 differ, so nothing is folded,
    and NEITHER is zero -- a row a do-nothing default could also produce asserts
    nothing.

    H7 SPOT CONTROL for this batch, measured 2026-08-17 by pulling ONE member's
    registration -- the minus entry -- from incant setup, read at run time so no
    rebuild is involved. Recorded in the commit; the shape is always the same:
    the count drops by exactly one and THE VALUES DO NOT MOVE.

    ⚠ OUTPUT FIELD INITIALIZED (bear-trap 26). JITTED HALF FIRST (bear-trap 25).
    ONE testing call then jitRefire, never two.
*****************************************************************************/

register(JitLadder);
define
    jsdA;
    jsdB;
    jsdC;
    jsdD;
    jsdZ;
    jsdOut;
    jsdAll code={
        jsdOut = jsdA + jsdB;
        jsdOut = jsdOut - jsdC;
        jsdOut = jsdOut / jsdD;
        if jsdOut != jsdZ;  jsdOut = 99;
        };
    ;

jsdA = 20; jsdB = 10; jsdC = 6; jsdD = 2; jsdZ = 12; jsdOut = 0;
print "JSD fire 1 setup : 20 10 6 2 vs 12":;
testing(jsdAll);
print "JSD fire 1 result: jsdOut =" jsdOut " (want 12)":;

jsdA = 40; jsdB = 10; jsdC = 5; jsdD = 5; jsdZ = 12; jsdOut = 0;
print "JSD fire 2 setup : 40 10 5 5 vs 12 (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JSD fire 2 result: jsdOut =" jsdOut " (want 99)":;

jsdA = 20; jsdB = 10; jsdC = 6; jsdD = 2; jsdZ = 12; jsdOut = 0;
jsdAll();
print "JSD interpreted  : jsdOut =" jsdOut " (the oracle -- want 12)":;
print "JITSLOT4 SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
